    "title": "fix(dflash2): keep sampling parameters out of the rotating UVA pool",
    "url": "https://github.com/1CatAI/1Cat-vLLM/pull/1442",
    "body": "## Symptom\n\nWith DFlash2 speculative decoding and a sampling temperature above zero, generation degenerates into repeating one fragment forever. At `T == 0` everything looks healthy.\n\nThat asymmetry is the fingerprint: greedy decoding never reads the temperature value, so the bug is invisible there.\n\n## Root cause\n\nSampling parameters live in UVA-backed tensors. `UvaBackedTensor.copy_to_uva()` hands back a buffer from a round-robin pool, so `.gpu` is a **different address after every** `apply_staged_writes()`. A CUDA graph captured against `self.temperature.gpu` keeps reading the buffer it saw at capture time — a stale slot with another request's parameters, or the zero-initialised default.\n\nA temperature of 0.0 is not merely \"greedy\": `logits / temperature` becomes inf and the distribution collapses to a point mass on the argmax, which is exactly the repeated fragment observed.\n\nThere is a second, independent trap. The `if not (do_top_k or do_top_p): return logits` style early exits are **CPU control flow**, so they are trace constants for `torch.cuda.graph`. Capture runs during warm-up, when every request still holds the default parameters, so \"skip\" got baked into the graph permanently — every later replay sampled an untempered distribution.\n\n## Fix\n\n- `SamplingStates` keeps graph-stable mirrors (`temperature`, `top_p`, `min_p`, `top_k`, `seeds`) at fixed addresses, refreshed alongside the rotating pool. Kernel launches read the mirrors.\n- A `capture_mode` flag, set only while capturing, forces the parametric kernels into the graph so the skip-branch cannot be baked in.\n- `temperature` is initialised to 1.0 rather than the UVA zero-init.\n- The rejection graph keeps its own copies and refreshes them in `prepare()` every round, including `cu_num_logits`.\n\n### Two details that cost time\n\n**Mirror sizing.** They must be sized to the *full* state table (`max_num_reqs`), not to the request count. The kernels index them with global request-slot ids via `batch.idx_mapping`; a table sized to the batch read out of bounds whenever a request lived in a high slot, which flipped `flags` inconsistently across TP ranks and deadlocked graph dispatch:\n\n```\nNo available shared memory broadcast block found in 60 seconds\n```\n\n**Seeding.** They are seeded with neutral values (temperature 1.0, top_p 1.0), not left as `empty_like`. `flags` is derived from them *during capture*, and uninitialised device memory produced a garbage branch decision. An earlier attempt that used `empty_like` deadlocked for this reason.\n\n## Also included\n\n`decode_step_profile.py` — `VLLM_DECODE_STEP_PROFILE=1` splits a decode round into per-segment CPU and GPU timings. Diagnostic, off by default, no behaviour change when disabled.\n\n## Testing\n\n- `T >= 0.5` free-form output no longer repeats; `T == 0` unchanged.\n- 1/2/4/8 concurrent requests, coding and free-form prompts.\n- Graph dispatch no longer deadlocks under concurrency; GPU power stays balanced across cards.",
